**Definition 1.2 (One fixed scalar port).** Let $\mathfrak C_1$ consist
of all composites of fixed CPTP maps

$$
\operatorname{Enc}:M_5(\mathbb C)\longrightarrow
\bigoplus_{f\in F}M_1(\mathbb C),\qquad
\operatorname{Dec}((y_f)_f)=\sum_f\operatorname{Dec}_f(y_f),\qquad
\operatorname{Dec}_f:M_1(\mathbb C)\longrightarrow M_5(\mathbb C),
\tag{1.2}
$$

where $F$ is finite and each decoder is CPTP on its whole block.
The design precedes the input and the future policy. All quantum
information traverses the stated block, without original-input
retention, entangled assistance or bypass. Flags are free for this
quantum-dimension criterion, are used in decoding, and are not
additionally supplied to the future policy. All complex CPTP
competitors are included. Set

$$
e(T)=\sup_{\rho\in\mathcal D(\mathbb C^5)}
\sup_{\pi\ {\rm actual,finite}}
\operatorname{TV}(P_\rho^\pi,P_{T(\rho)}^\pi),\qquad
\varepsilon_1=\inf_{T\in\mathfrak C_1}e(T).
\tag{1.3}
$$

Replacement occurs once before the common protocol. TV is half the
$\ell^1$ distance, with its probability-measure version for random
controls. Complete positivity includes arbitrary reference extensions;
the performance criterion measures system histories only.

